    annotation: `This article explains how the Biden administration changed U.S. border and migration policy when the COVID-era Title 42 order was at its end. Title 42 allowed the government to expel migrants quickly, normally without any asylum screenings. This was more than 2.8 million times and with border encounters at an all time high, the administration expected that reverting back to the old immigration policies would overwhelm the Department of Homeland Security. The article mainly argues that the administration responded to this by restructuring migration through two strategies. One to create lawful pathways and to expand work visas. The other was to promise to double the Western Hemisphere refugee admissions, while also penalizing irregular crossing through a rule that presumed most migrants who entered Mexico without documents were ineligible for asylum. This article supports this with Federal Register rules, official statements, and Custom and Border Protection data. It also presents criticism from the UN Refugee Agency, the International Organization for Migration, and The American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU). This individual source relates to my research questions because it helps explain the changes in refugee and asylum categories in our dataset during the years of 2023 and 2024. It also reveals the gap in the data that people who were admitted through the parole programs entered only temporarily. This means that those people don’t appear in the permanent residents, refugee, or asylee counts. This source is important because it explains how Title 42 replaced normal immigration procedures during the pandemic, something most discussions of refugee and asylum policy leave out. In relation to my thesis, this source directly answers questions about COVID-19. Because Title 42 expelled people before they could apply for asylum, low asylee numbers from 2020 to 2022 may reflect a public health order, not only an administration's asylum policy. It is also hard to credit a certain administration for these asylum trends during those years because Title 42 was used under both the Trump and Biden administrations. Another thing this article notes is that there were many migrants that were admitted under the Biden administration that come through parole programs, and because they are not counted as refugees or asylees, our dataset will understate how many people the Biden administration actually admitted. One limitation that this article had was that it was written weeks after the policies took effect meaning it relies heavily on the administration's own statements. It cannot show whether the policies worked overtime.`,
