    annotation: `In this article the author examines how U.S. immigration enforcement separated migrant children from their parents under both the Obama and Trump administrations. The author argues that family separation didn’t begin or end with Trump’s 2018 “zero tolerance” policy. Also that both administrations had mistakes that built on each other until separations became widespread. Under Obama most families caught crossing the border were processed in civil court rather than prosecuted, asylum seekers and families were generally exempt from prosecutions, and families seeking asylum were detained together and later released with ankle monitors while their claims were processed. The Obama admissions also expanded family detention and that its Secure Communities program separated tens of thousands of families. Under the “zero tolerance” policy, the Trump administration prosecuted every adult who crossed between the ports of entry, with no exception for asylum seekers. This is what led children to be in separated custody. Court cases like Flores and Ms. L v. ICE, government reports and statistics, statements from the American Psychological Association, and the American Medical Association support the author’s claims. The author concludes his article by calling for alternatives to family detention, an independent immigration court, and legislation protecting families at the border. This source is important to our thesis because it challenges the idea that the transition from Obama to Trump was a complete break, and shows that harsh border practices were in effect for both administrations. Also this article shows that the Trump administration removed asylum exemptions, ended a supervised release program for asylum-seeking families in 2017, and in 2019 began requiring asylum seekers to wait in Mexico during their cases. These policies that Trump put in place could explain the differences in asylee numbers during the two administrations. Although the articles do not show any numeric data on asylum grants, the time in which the policies were put into place could explain certain trends relating to asylees in our data. This also supports the focus of this project about data and silence. The author does report that the Obama administration did not collect data on family separations and that the Trump administration did not record which families had been separated, so the families are not recorded in official statistics. One limitation this article has in relation to our project is that it is advocacy-oriented and that it doesn’t address the Biden administration.`,
