    annotation: `This source argues that during its first year, the Trump administration used executive action to restrict both legal and unauthorized immigration more than any modern administration. This is also while the courts, Congress, and state governments tried to slow down this process. This article supports these claims with the administration’s executive orders, enforcement data from Immigration and Customs Enforcement, and refugee admissions data from the State Department. The most important section from this article in relation to our research is the refugee section. It shows that the Obama administration actually raised the refugee ceiling from 70,000 from 2013-2015 to 85,000 in 2016. They then rose to 110,000 in 2017 in response to the global refugee crisis. However, after taking office Trump suspended the refugee program for 120 days and cut Obama’s 2017 number to 60,000, which only resulted in 53,176 admissions that entire year. Admissions were even lower the following year as the ceiling was at 45,000, which was the lowest it’s been since the Refugee Act of 1980. This article also describes extra vetting for refugees from eleven countries that the Trump administration deemed high-risk. This sharply reduced admissions from these countries. It also suspended entries for family members of refugees already in the United States. This source is important to this project because it documented these changes with official figures as they happened. It also separates what the administration promised from what it actually carried out. This answers our thesis by explaining why refugee admissions fell after the 2017 transition. The article also shows that 2017 is a mixed year as this was the transition year. This means that the ceiling was set under Obama and then cut under Trump partway through. Due to the article only reporting national figures, our state-level data is needed to show which states saw the largest declines and whether those declines matched the national pattern. One limitation that this article has is that it was written after only one year of the Trump administration, so it cannot show if refugee admissions stayed low in the later years of the Trump administration. It also focuses on federal policy rather than the outcomes of the states. It says that there is a decline, but doesn't show where the decline was the largest.`,
