      'Ryo, Emily. “The Unintended Consequences of US Immigration Enforcement Policies.” *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*, vol. 118, no. 21, 2021, article e2103000118. [https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2103000118](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2103000118).',
    annotation: `Ryo argues that the punitive U.S. enforcement policies meant to deter unauthorized migration, immigration detention and “nonjudicial removals” (deportations without a hearing before an immigration judge), do not change whether people plan to migrate to the United States, but that detention in particular damages how fair potential migrants believe the U.S. immigration system is. Her evidence comes from a randomized experiment that she designed with the Latin American Public Opinion Project and placed on the 2018/19 AmericasBarometer, a nationally representative survey of voting-age adults. About 6,100 respondents in Mexico, El Salvador, Guatemala, and Honduras, the four largest sources of unauthorized migration to the U.S., were randomly given one of three short statements: (a) U.S. authorities had apprehended thousands of their fellow citizens, (b) those people were also held in detention for days, weeks, or months, or (c) they were removed without seeing a judge. Plans to migrate were nearly identical across all three groups, with about 58 to 60 percent saying they were “not at all likely” to go to the U.S. in the next three years, and the result held for people with U.S. family ties, crime victimization, or low incomes. However, respondents told about detention were significantly more likely to see the system’s procedures and outcomes as unfair. This study is important because it directly tests the assumption behind increasingly harsh enforcement, that “tougher policies discourage people from migrating in the first place,” instead of taking it for granted, and its experimental design allows causal conclusions that earlier observational studies could not make. It also shows that people do not need to experience detention personally to lose trust in U.S. law. For our project, the study suggests that changes in asylum grants between the Obama, Trump, and Biden administrations may reflect U.S. policy decisions about who is approved more than changes in who wants to come, since enforcement alone does not seem to reduce migration. This matters for our research question about refugee and asylee admissions across administrations, because the Trump administration expanded detention explicitly as a deterrent. However, the study has limitations for our purposes. It measures intentions to migrate rather than actual asylum applications or grants, and Ryo admits the brief survey statements may have been too weak to produce a deterrent effect. It also covers only Mexico and Central America, so it speaks more to our data on asylum grants than to refugee arrivals, who are mostly resettled from overseas.`,
