    annotation: `Samari, Nagle, and Coleman-Minahan argue that U.S. states have built a largely exclusionary immigration policy climate, which they call “structural xenophobia,” and that this climate is a form of structural racism that shapes immigrant health. To measure it, they created the Immigration Policy Climate (IPC) index from 714 coded state policies across all 50 states and D.C. from 2009 to 2019, using state legislative records and policy databases from foundations, advocacy groups, and scholars. The index covers fourteen policies in five areas: public health and welfare benefits, higher education, labor and employment, driver’s licenses and identification, and immigration enforcement. Each policy is coded as inclusive (1), neutral (0), or exclusive (-1), for example whether a state offers in-state tuition to undocumented students or requires employers to use E-Verify, giving each state a yearly score from -12 to 12. The authors find that two-thirds of state policies were exclusionary and the national mean score was -2.5. Over the decade, thirty-three states were exclusionary on average, seven were neutral, and eleven were inclusive, with Georgia, Alabama, and Indiana the most exclusionary and Oregon, Washington, and California the most inclusive. About 75 percent of states changed by four points or less, and no state moved from largely exclusionary to largely inclusive, although some states shifted toward inclusion between 2016 and 2019 in response to the Trump administration. This study is important because it is one of the few measures that tracks both welcoming and restrictive state policies over a full decade, rather than a single year or only harsh laws. It also makes a key distinction for our project in that federal law decides who can enter the country, while states decide immigrants’ access to rights and resources once they arrive. For our project, the IPC index gives us a published, comparable score for how welcoming each state was during most of our dataset’s years (2013 to 2019). We could compare these scores with states’ refugee arrivals, asylum grants, and naturalization rates per capita to see whether states that “resist” or “welcome” immigrants look different in our data, which connects directly to our question about state political leaning. However, the index has limitations for our purposes. As the authors note, it records when laws passed rather than how they were enforced. It also ended before COVID-19 and the Biden administration, and because the federal government rather than states decides admissions, any link between a state’s climate and its admission numbers is indirect.`,
