    annotation: `Sanderson, Semyonov, and Gorodzeisky argue that Americans’ opposition to immigration declined steadily from 1996 to 2018, but that the decline happened mostly among Democrats, which made immigration an increasingly divisive political issue between the parties. Their evidence comes from two nationally representative surveys, nine waves of the General Social Survey and seven waves of the American National Election Studies. Both asked whether the number of immigrants permitted to come to the United States should be increased or decreased. In 1996, 64.6 percent (GSS) and 58.9 percent (ANES) of Americans opposed increasing immigration, but by 2018 this had fallen to 34.7 and 33.1 percent. Using multilevel models, the authors show the decline held after controlling for education, age, gender, race, employment, immigrant status, ideology, and region, and that it was not explained by younger generations replacing older ones. The trend was also steady and linear, no single event during the period, including the 2008 recession or changes in presidential policy, reversed or sped it up. When the authors separated respondents by party, they found that Republicans’ opposition barely changed, while Democrats’ opposition fell sharply, with Independents in between, so the gap between the parties widened over two decades. This study is important because it challenges the “competitive threat” theory predicting that rising immigrant populations, economic downturns, and nationalist politics should increase opposition to immigration. Instead, opposition fell overall while splitting along party lines, which suggests that immigration attitudes are increasingly tied to political identity. The study provides context for our question about whether Republican- and Democratic-leaning states drifted further apart on immigration after 2017. If public opinion was already divided by party before 2017, then differences we find between states in our 2013 to 2022 data may show a longer trend rather than a sudden change under the Trump administration. It also pairs well with Samari et al., since a growing partisan divide in public opinion could help explain why some states passed more inclusive policies while others became more exclusionary. However, the study has limitations for our purposes, measuring national public opinion rather than differences between states or actual admissions, and the authors could only control for nine broad regions rather than individual states. It also ends in 2018, covering only the first two years of the Trump administration, and the authors note that their measure of “opposition” may capture a wide range of values beyond anti-immigrant sentiment.`,
