    annotation: `Costa studied how temporary work visas have been increasingly dominating the U.S. labor immigration system, while the number of employment-based permanent immigrants has remained relatively stable. He compared these two pathways using government visa data from 1987 to 2017. During this period, the proportion of permanent immigrant workers among new immigrant workers dropped from approximately 20% to less than 10%. Costa pointed out that this shift did not occur through a major immigration legislative reform. His research indicates that the number of people granted the right to work in the U.S. does not necessarily reflect the number of people who have gained the opportunity for permanent settlement. This paper is very useful because in general immigration discussions, temporary workers and permanent residents are often discussed together, but their legal situations are actually different. Obtaining a work permit does not always mean there is a clear path to a green card. This distinction is very important when interpreting immigration statistics, as different categories represent different rights and opportunities. The increase in temporary work visas may occur simultaneously with the slow growth of employment-based permanent immigrants. For our group's research, Costa provided the background for understanding green cards and adjustment of status. Before comparing the number of immigrants under different presidential administrations, we need to first explain what each category actually measures. A person who obtains permanent resident status through adjustment of status may have already been living in the U.S. If this identity adjustment is counted as a new entry, it can be misleading. Moreover, permanent resident data cannot cover all those who have worked on temporary visas. Therefore, this article can help us clarify who is included in the dataset and what groups it cannot present. The longer research time frame of this article is also an advantage. It reminds us that when discussing a particular administration, we need to first check whether the relevant trends have already begun. Changes that occurred after 2017 may be related to long-term trends rather than solely caused by new policies of that year. Therefore, when linking chart changes to presidential leadership, we need to be cautious. However, this article cannot explain all aspects of our project. Its data is up to 2017, so it does not cover COVID-19 or the Biden administration. It mainly discusses labor immigration at the national level, rather than interstate differences, refugee admissions, or asylum decisions. These trends cannot directly prove which policies caused the changes in our data. I will mainly use this paper to explain immigration categories and provide historical background. As for changes in specific states or years, we still need other evidence.`,
